1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Retiring software isn't proving it's gone.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:08,000
A forgotten CI job can bring a dead service back.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:13,000
Termivance makes retirement a proof-bound protocol - and here, it's blocking an unsafe retirement on purpose.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:19,000
Termivance separates three questions: is retirement safe, is absence actually observed, and can the resource come back?

5
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Each needs different proof.

6
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:30,000
A GitLab Duo agent investigates - but deterministic Python alone decides, authorizes, and proves.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,000
A human asked for retirement on a neutral GitLab issue.

8
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:43,000
A real Duo Custom Flow picked it up, read the target repository,

9
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:50,000
and found a surviving create-pages job that could republish the site - posting its findings as non-authoritative candidates.

10
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:56,000
Termivance then checks every AI claim against separate GitLab provider facts.

11
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:02,000
Four candidates were corroborated, two stayed unresolved, one was contextual.

12
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:08,000
AI suggestions never become facts without deterministic verification.

13
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Now the deterministic engine runs live.

14
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,000
It re-reads the target, evaluates eight retirement obligations, and refuses: safe-to-retire is blocked.

15
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Watch the safety refusal: a real mutation demands an independent operator trust policy.

16
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:40,000
AI recommendations cannot authorize deletion - and neither can stale evidence.

17
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:45,500
The target is real: a GitLab Pages site,

18
00:01:45,500 --> 00:01:50,500
publicly serving its unique marker right now - we just loaded it live.

19
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:56,000
Published by its own CI, which is exactly the resurrection risk. Presence is not retirement.

20
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:01,500
The obligation graph shows what blocks retirement: GitLab Pages administration answered HTTP four-oh-three, forbidden;

21
00:02:01,500 --> 00:02:06,500
the redeployment path is active; and no operator authorization exists.

22
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:14,000
Clicking the CI node reveals the exact recorded evidence behind each edge.

23
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:19,000
Even hypothetically, projections are labeled projected-not-observed.

24
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:24,500
Termivance proposes bounded repair steps -

25
00:02:24,500 --> 00:02:30,000
and evidence expires after fifteen minutes, so stale proof can never authorize anything.

26
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:36,000
Every push runs real GitLab CI: five hundred sixty-three tests and forty safety assertions.

27
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:41,000
An independent GitHub observer checks live GitLab APIs

28
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:46,000
and confirms the exact source revision - pass, in sync.

29
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:54,000
Termivance: software retirement, proven within bounds.
